      "question": {
        ":question/id": "2f89e2ed-43cf-53b7-a298-8d0a21cdd815",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the definite integral $\\int_{-2}^{0}\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{3} + 9}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x^{3} + 9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =3 x^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =3 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u & =x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^{3} + 9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -2 & 0 \\\\ u & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^{0}\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{3} + 9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\ln(u)|_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\ln(9)-\\ln(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\log{\\left(3 \\right)}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
