        ":question/problem": "Calculate the definite integral $\\int_{-1}^{1}\\frac{x^{2}}{4 x^{3} + 5}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =4 x^{3} + 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =12 x^{2} \\\\ \\text{d}u & =12 x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{d}u & =x^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=4 x^{3} + 5.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & -1 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1}\\frac{x^{2}}{4 x^{3} + 5}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{12}\\int_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\ln(u)|_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(\\ln(9)-\\ln(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\log{\\left(3 \\right)}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
